    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d15">

                <pb facs="0032" n="22" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg22"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European Advisory Commission/60:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-24T16:00:00+01:00">January 24, 1944—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>632. Comea 17. The British have heard from their Embassy at Washington that a new
                    scheme is under advisement there for the treatment of the French civil affairs
                    question, and that this scheme would make unnecessary the clearance of the
                    existing “basic scheme” by the Advisory Commission (see my 544, January 20, 9
                    p.m., Comea 16<anchor n="42" xml:id="d15-f1"/>).</p>
                <p>They have therefore dropped their intention of bringing the existing paper before
                    the Commission at the next meeting, and are awaiting further clarification of
                    the matter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d15-f1" n="42" xml:id="d15-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. This telegram stated that the British wished to bring the document
                    entitled “Civil Affairs in France” before the next meeting of the European
                    Advisory Commission; the Ambassador requested further instructions on the
                    subject (740.00119 European Advisory Commission/56).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
